A legal AHB incrementing burst never crosses a 1KB boundary (IHI0033B.b §3.5), and every 4KB boundary is also a 1KB boundary, so the AXI bursts made from it never cross 4KB: no 4KB split is needed. The bridge splits because an AXI4 INCR burst carries at most 256 transfers (IHI0022E A3.4.1), while an undefined-length AHB INCR has no length limit of its own; a byte-wide INCR can carry 1024 transfers inside one 1KB region.
```
while (remaining > 0) begin
  n = (remaining > 256) ? 256 : remaining;  // AXI4 INCR limit
  // AWADDR = addr, AWLEN = n - 1, AWSIZE = HSIZE, AWBURST = INCR
  // send n W transfers, WLAST on the n-th
  addr      += n << size;
  remaining -= n;
end
```
Width conversion is the other reason to reshape: a 64-bit AHB transfer on a 32-bit AXI bus becomes two AXI transfers, and a downsized WRAP16 needs two INCR bursts because AXI WRAP stops at 16 transfers. Correlate W transfers and B responses per split burst, and complete the AHB write only after every split's BRESP if the bridge is non-posted. An AHB burst that crosses 1KB is illegal stimulus: flag it with an AHB-side checker rather than splitting it. The 1KB split belongs to the other direction: an AXI-to-AHB bridge must split a legal AXI INCR at each 1KB boundary.
